module frcp.periods

import core.authority.{ CaseOrder }
import core.frap26.{ frap26 }
import core.frcp6.{ frcp6 }
import core.time.{ ServiceMethod }

// The periods the pack's edges run on. Each is counted by a `lit/core`
// counting rule: FRCP 6(a) for the civil rules and for statutes that state no
// method of their own (FRCP 6(a) reaches "any statute that does not specify a
// method of computing time"), FRAP 26(a) for the FRAP 4 appeal clocks.
//
// Effective dates. A period's current version is in force from the latest
// amendment of its rule that the vendored text's history line records
// (`sources/frcp.txt` "(As amended ...)" notes; `sources/usc-cfr-prosecution-
// fragments.txt` for 28 U.S.C. § 1447). Where the vendored text carries no
// history (FRAP 4, 28 U.S.C. § 1446) the period is in force from the package
// default, December 1, 2024, the date the pack's sources state the text as
// of; it is a lower bound, never an invented enactment date.
//
// Delegation. `delegable_to [CaseOrder]` marks a period the court may change
// in one case: where the rule's own text says so ("unless the court sets a
// different time", "unless the court orders otherwise", "unless a different
// time is set by stipulation or court order"), and where the pack records an
// FRCP 6(b)(1) extension ("the court may, for good cause, extend the time").
// FRCP 6(b)(2) forbids extending the Rule 50(b), 59(b), 59(e) and 60(b)
// periods, and the pack marks the removal, remand-motion, waiver, Rule
// 15(a)(1) and cross-appeal periods unextendable, so they delegate nothing.
/// The complaint is filed (FRCP 3): the Rule 4(m) service clock starts.
event ComplaintFiled()
/// The summons and complaint are served under Rule 4. Service of process is
/// never Rule 5(b) service, so FRCP 6(d) adds no days to periods it starts.
event ProcessServed()
/// The plaintiff sends a Rule 4(d)(1) waiver request.
event WaiverRequestSent()
/// The defendant receives the initial pleading, "through service or
/// otherwise" (28 U.S.C. § 1446(b)(1); FRCP 81(c)(2)(A)).
event InitialPleadingReceived()
/// A notice of removal is filed under 28 U.S.C. § 1446(a).
event NoticeOfRemovalFiled()
/// The court denies a Rule 12 motion or postpones its disposition until
/// trial (FRCP 12(a)(4)(A) runs from notice of that action).
event Rule12MotionDenied()
/// A responsive pleading is served, by `method` (FRCP 5(b)).
event ResponsivePleadingServed(method: ServiceMethod)
/// An amended pleading is served, by `method` (FRCP 5(b)).
event AmendedPleadingServed(method: ServiceMethod)
/// The last pleading directed to a jury-triable issue is served, by
/// `method` (FRCP 38(b)(1)).
event LastPleadingServed(method: ServiceMethod)
/// The parties hold their Rule 26(f) conference.
event Rule26fConferenceHeld()
/// Judgment is entered (FRCP 58); the post-trial and appeal clocks run from
/// entry.
event JudgmentEntered()
/// The first notice of appeal is filed (FRAP 4(a)(3)).
event NoticeOfAppealFiled()

/// Rule 4(m): 90 days after the complaint is filed; on good cause the court
/// "must extend the time for service", and absent it may "order that
/// service be made within a specified time". Rule 4 last amended eff.
/// December 1, 2017.
deadline service-period: 90.days after ComplaintFiled() counted_by frcp6 effective [2017-12-01, ..) delegable_to [CaseOrder] cite FRCP 4(m)
/// Rule 12(a)(1)(A)(ii): an answer is due 60 days after a timely returned
/// waiver's request was sent (90 days for a defendant outside every judicial
/// district, which the pack does not branch on). Rule 12 last amended eff.
/// December 1, 2024.
deadline waiver-answer-period: 60.days after WaiverRequestSent() counted_by frcp6 effective [2024-12-01, ..) cite FRCP 12(a)(1)(A)(ii)
/// Rule 12(a)(1)(A)(i): an answer is due 21 days after service of the
/// summons and complaint; a pre-answer Rule 12(b) motion "must be made
/// before pleading", so it runs on the same clock. Extendable under FRCP
/// 6(b)(1). Rule 12 last amended eff. December 1, 2024.
deadline answer-period: 21.days after ProcessServed() counted_by frcp6 effective [2024-12-01, ..) delegable_to [CaseOrder] cite FRCP 12(a)(1)(A)(i)
/// 28 U.S.C. § 1446(b)(1): a notice of removal is due 30 days after the
/// defendant receives the initial pleading. A statute: no court may extend
/// it. The vendored section carries no history, so it is dated from the
/// package default.
deadline removal-period: 30.days after InitialPleadingReceived() counted_by frcp6 effective [2024-12-01, ..) cite 28 U.S.C. 1446(b)(1)
/// 28 U.S.C. § 1447(c): a motion to remand for any defect other than lack
/// of subject-matter jurisdiction is due 30 days after the notice of
/// removal is filed. Section 1447 last amended by Pub. L. 112-51, § 2(d),
/// November 9, 2011 (vendored history).
deadline remand-motion-period: 30.days after NoticeOfRemovalFiled() counted_by frcp6 effective [2011-11-09, ..) cite 28 U.S.C. 1447(c)
/// Rule 81(c)(2)(A): after removal, a defendant who has not answered
/// answers 21 days after receiving the initial pleading (the longest of
/// three periods; the pack models this one). Extendable under FRCP 6(b)(1).
/// Rule 81 last amended eff. December 1, 2009.
deadline removed-answer-period: 21.days after InitialPleadingReceived() counted_by frcp6 effective [2009-12-01, ..) delegable_to [CaseOrder] cite FRCP 81(c)(2)(A)
/// Rule 12(a)(4)(A): "Unless the court sets a different time", the
/// responsive pleading is due 14 days after notice that the court denied
/// the Rule 12 motion. Rule 12 last amended eff. December 1, 2024.
deadline denial-answer-period: 14.days after Rule12MotionDenied() counted_by frcp6 effective [2024-12-01, ..) delegable_to [CaseOrder] cite FRCP 12(a)(4)(A)
/// Rule 15(a)(1)(B): amendment once as a matter of course no later than 21
/// days after service of a responsive pleading (or of a Rule 12(b), (e) or
/// (f) motion, whichever is earlier). Rule 15 last amended eff. December 1,
/// 2023.
deadline amendment-as-of-course-period: 21.days after ResponsivePleadingServed() counted_by frcp6 effective [2023-12-01, ..) cite FRCP 15(a)(1)(B)
/// Rule 15(a)(3): "Unless the court orders otherwise", a response to an
/// amended pleading is due within the time left on the original or 14 days
/// after service of the amendment, whichever is later; the pack models the
/// 14 days. Rule 15 last amended eff. December 1, 2023.
deadline amended-response-period: 14.days after AmendedPleadingServed() counted_by frcp6 effective [2023-12-01, ..) delegable_to [CaseOrder] cite FRCP 15(a)(3)
/// Rule 26(a)(1)(C): initial disclosures are due 14 days after the Rule
/// 26(f) conference "unless a different time is set by stipulation or court
/// order". Rule 26 last amended eff. December 1, 2015.
deadline initial-disclosure-period: 14.days after Rule26fConferenceHeld() counted_by frcp6 effective [2015-12-01, ..) delegable_to [CaseOrder] cite FRCP 26(a)(1)(C)
/// Rule 38(b)(1): a jury demand is due 14 days after the last pleading
/// directed to the issue is served. Extendable under FRCP 6(b)(1). Rule 38
/// last amended eff. December 1, 2009.
deadline jury-demand-period: 14.days after LastPleadingServed() counted_by frcp6 effective [2009-12-01, ..) delegable_to [CaseOrder] cite FRCP 38(b)(1)
/// Rule 50(b): a renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law is due 28
/// days after entry of judgment; FRCP 6(b)(2) forbids extending it. Rule 50
/// last amended eff. December 1, 2009.
deadline jmol-renewal-period: 28.days after JudgmentEntered() counted_by frcp6 effective [2009-12-01, ..) cite FRCP 50(b)
/// Rule 59(b): a new-trial motion is due 28 days after entry of judgment;
/// FRCP 6(b)(2) forbids extending it. Rule 59 last amended eff. December 1,
/// 2009.
deadline new-trial-period: 28.days after JudgmentEntered() counted_by frcp6 effective [2009-12-01, ..) cite FRCP 59(b)
/// Rule 59(e): a motion to alter or amend the judgment is due 28 days after
/// its entry; FRCP 6(b)(2) forbids extending it. Rule 59 last amended eff.
/// December 1, 2009.
deadline alter-amend-period: 28.days after JudgmentEntered() counted_by frcp6 effective [2009-12-01, ..) cite FRCP 59(e)
/// Rule 60(c)(1): a Rule 60(b)(1)-(3) motion is due "no more than a year
/// after the entry of the judgment", a calendar year (`frcp6`'s
/// `end_of_month clamp` ends one from February 29 on February 28); FRCP
/// 6(b)(2) forbids extending it. Rule 60 last amended eff. December 1, 2007.
deadline relief-from-judgment-period: 1.years after JudgmentEntered() counted_by frcp6 effective [2007-12-01, ..) cite FRCP 60(c)(1)
/// FRAP 4(a)(1)(A): the notice of appeal is due 30 days after entry of
/// judgment (28 U.S.C. § 2107(a)); the district court may extend it under
/// FRAP 4(a)(5) and 28 U.S.C. § 2107(c).
deadline notice-of-appeal-period: 30.days after JudgmentEntered() counted_by frap26 effective [2024-12-01, ..) delegable_to [CaseOrder] cite FRAP 4(a)(1)(A)
/// FRAP 4(a)(3): a cross-appeal is due 14 days after the first notice of
/// appeal is filed (or within the FRAP 4(a)(1) time, whichever ends later;
/// the pack models the 14 days).
deadline cross-appeal-period: 14.days after NoticeOfAppealFiled() counted_by frap26 effective [2024-12-01, ..) cite FRAP 4(a)(3)
